  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nThe primary forecast focus and challenge remains timing and\nlocation of periods of heavy rain through Sunday.\n\nLow clouds continue to slowly scatter out across southern Lake\nMichigan and into far northeast IL early this morning. This\ntrend is expected to continue inland this morning though there\nwill likely be mid level cloud cover later today. Net result\nshould be at least some peaks of sunshine today. A weak wave may \nbring a few showers to western and northwest IL today, but much\nof the area is expected to remain dry. High temperatures today \nare little tricky, generally in the lower 70s north to upper 70s\nsouth but if enough sunshine materializes, these temps could be\na few to possibly several degrees too cool. Northeast winds \nwill allow waves to build into the 3 to 6 foot range at IL Lake \nMichigan beaches and the current beach hazard statement looks \non track. East/southeast winds will increase again overnight \ninto Saturday morning and another beach hazard statement may be\nneeded.\n\nAttention then shifts to what is expected to be developing\nconvection across northern IA and southwest WI later this\nevening which is then expected to spread into northern IL early\nSaturday morning. Still some uncertainty for timing but the time\nperiod of concern seems to be focused on 09z to 15z Saturday.\nWith precipitable water values during this time in the 2 inch\nrange, very heavy rain is expected and if it trains over the \nsame areas, flooding and localized flash flooding will be \npossible. While a flood watch may be need for parts of the area,\nthis time period is still 24 hours away and there remains \nuncertainly for how far south into the cwa a flood watch may be \nneeded. Therefore, no flood watch headline is planned this \nmorning. \n\nTrends for Saturday afternoon into Saturday night will likely be\ndetermined with how the convection Saturday morning materializes\nand there is some concern that additional thunderstorms will\nredevelop in the afternoon though there is quite a bit of\nuncertainty. SPC Day2 outlook has a level 1 severe risk for\nlocalized damaging winds for most of the area on Saturday. \nAnother period of heavy rain is expected Saturday night into \nSunday morning, but confidence for where this sets up is low. \nThough if it does take aim a the local area, then another flood\nwatch could potentially be needed during this time period as \nwell.\n\nAs winds turn northeast Monday into Tuesday, cooler air will\nspread across the region with high temperatures possibly only\nreaching the lower/mid 60s both days, well below normal for mid\nSeptember. Persistent and at times, strong northeast winds will\nallow waves to build along the IL/IN Lake Michigan shore with \nhigh waves and dangerous currents expected during this time \nperiod. cms",
